("note", "DEFINITION", "A **quadratic equation** in x is one of the form **ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c = 0**, where a, b, c are real and **a &ne; 0**. Its solutions are called **roots** (zeroes of the polynomial); it has **at most two roots**."),
("p", "Equations such as (x &minus; 2)<sup>2</sup> + 1 = 2x &minus; 3 reduce to x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 6x + 8 = 0, which is quadratic; x(x + 1) + 8 = (x + 2)(x &minus; 2) reduces to x + 12 = 0, which is **not** quadratic."),
("h2", "4.2 Solution by Factorisation"),
("steps", [("Step 1", "Write the equation as ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c = 0."), ("Step 2", "Split the middle term into two terms whose product is ac and sum is b."), ("Step 3", "Factorise and equate each factor to zero.")]),
("p", "**Example.** 2x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 5x + 3 = 0: split &minus;5x = &minus;2x &minus; 3x: 2x(x &minus; 1) &minus; 3(x &minus; 1) = (2x &minus; 3)(x &minus; 1) = 0. Roots **3/2 and 1**. Another: x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 3x &minus; 10 = (x &minus; 5)(x + 2) = 0; roots 5 and &minus;2."),
("h2", "4.3 Completing the Square and the Formula"),
("p", "**Example (completing the square).** x<sup>2</sup> + 6x &minus; 7 = 0: add 9 to both sides after moving the constant: x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9 = 16, so (x + 3)<sup>2</sup> = 16, x + 3 = &plusmn;4, giving **x = 1 or x = &minus;7**."),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Quadratic formula:** x = [&minus;b &plusmn; &radic;(b<sup>2</sup> &minus; 4ac)] / 2a, valid when b<sup>2</sup> &minus; 4ac &ge; 0.  The expression **D = b<sup>2</sup> &minus; 4ac** is the **discriminant**."),
("p", "**Example.** x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 6x + 4 = 0: D = 36 &minus; 16 = 20, x = (6 &plusmn; 2 &radic;5)/2 = **3 &plusmn; &radic;5**. Also 2x<sup>2</sup> &minus; 7x + 3 = 0: D = 49 &minus; 24 = 25, x = (7 &plusmn; 5)/4 = 3 or 1/2."),
